    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and all tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change adds a computed `namesakeCount` on owner creation \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same first + last name (case-insensitive), captured before the new owner is saved and returned on subsequent GETs.\n\nFollowing the existing `Assign<E>` pattern already used for `customerCode` and `householdId`, I:\n\n1. **New function** `AssignOwnerNamesakeCount` \u2014 a small, single-responsibility step that counts matching existing owners via `OwnerRepository.findAll()` and sets the count on the built `Owner` in place. Because it runs before `save`, the count naturally excludes the owner being created.\n\n2. **Wired it into the pipeline** (`owners.POST.yml`) between `build` and `defaultRegistrationDate`, under the same `transaction` governance as the surrounding steps.\n\n3. **Persisted the value**: added a `namesakeCount` field/column to the `Owner` entity and to all four `schema.sql` variants (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres).\n\n4. **Exposed it in the API**: added a read-only `namesakeCount` property to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml` (auto-mapped by MapStruct in `toOwnerDto`), and told `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` to ignore it, consistent with the other derived fields.\n\nThe count uses a simple case-insensitive comparison local to the function, matching how the surrounding create steps read `findAll()` directly.",
